@factiii/runner: dual-use disclosure

WHAT THIS PACKAGE IS

Factiii Runner lets a person run AI coding agents (Claude Code and similar)
on a computer they own, and drive them from the Factiii web, desktop and
mobile apps (https://factiii.com). The owner installs it on their own machine
and pairs it with their own Factiii account. It then accepts work only from
that account and from board members the owner has approved.

Doing that job means the runner can do things that malware also does. Each
one is listed below with why it is there and how the owner controls it.
Nothing here runs at install: the package has no install scripts.


CAPABILITIES THAT RESEMBLE MALWARE

1. Remote shell and command execution
   The runner starts terminal sessions (tmux) and agent processes on the
   machine and streams them to the owner's Factiii app over WebRTC. That is
   the product: the owner, or an approved board member, types into a
   terminal on their own computer from a phone or browser. Some processes
   are spawned detached so that a running agent survives a runner restart.

2. iOS Simulator viewing and input (macOS)
   native/simhid.m and native/simstream.m are built on the owner's Mac and
   stream the iOS Simulator's video and send it touch input, for testing
   mobile apps. They reach the Simulator only, never the Mac's own screen,
   mouse or keyboard. The runner does not capture or control the Mac's
   desktop; to see it remotely the owner uses macOS Screen Sharing (VNC)
   over their own Tailscale network, which the runner neither installs nor
   starts.

3. Reverse tunnels (frpc, downloaded on first use)
   The package does not contain the frp client (https://github.com/fatedier/frp).
   The first time the owner or an agent shares a local dev server as a
   preview link (`factiii-expose`), the runner downloads frp's official
   release from GitHub, checks its SHA-256 against the value frp publishes
   (pinned in the runner's source), and refuses it if they differ. A runner
   that never shares a port never has frpc. Tunnels go only to Factiii's own
   tunnel server, with the runner's own access token. Each tunnel is listed
   in the app and can be stopped.

4. Starting at login (persistence)
   Off by default. Only the person at the machine turns it on, in its own
   terminal: by answering yes to setup's "Start this runner at login? [y/N]"
   (Enter alone means no), or by running `factiii-runner service install`.
   Either installs one per-user login service (a LaunchAgent on macOS, a
   systemd user unit on Linux, a scheduled task on Windows), so the runner is
   available after a reboot. The app and the account can see it and turn it
   off, never on: a request to install it from anywhere but that terminal is
   refused. `factiii-runner service remove` deletes it.
   The runner lists the LaunchAgents folder to find and manage its own
   entries, and reads the owner's other agents there only to name the
   services in item 10. It never adds, changes or removes another agent.

5. Credentials and secrets
   - The runner's connection token is stored encrypted, under a key kept in
     the OS keychain (macOS Keychain via `security`, libsecret on Linux,
     DPAPI on Windows).
   - The owner can keep deploy secrets in a local, encrypted store. They are
     handed to commands only through named jobs the owner defines, and each
     use is recorded in an audit log the owner can read.
   - The runner reads environment variables it is configured with (for
     example FACTIII_* settings). It never reads `.env` files, SSH keys,
     browser data, wallets or other programs' credentials. A test in this
     package (src/packageContents.test.ts) fails if bundled code opens a
     .env file.

6. System and remote-access inspection
   Runner > Status, and "Ready to be a server" in Factiii Desktop, show the
   owner whether their machine can serve as an always-on host. To do this the
   runner reads disk and memory use (/proc/meminfo on Linux), power settings
   (`pmset -g`), screen-lock and FileVault state (`sysadminctl`, `ioreg`),
   whether Screen Sharing is on (a local check of port 5900), and the
   machine's Tailscale address (`ifconfig`, `tailscale status`). These
   results go to the owner's own app.
   - The runner never changes these settings itself. Where a setting needs
     changing it shows the command, such as `sudo pmset ...`, for the owner
     to run, or the owner runs it from Factiii Desktop with their password.
   - "Keep awake" holds `caffeinate` while the owner asks for it.
   - The runner never installs or starts Tailscale. It only reads its status.

7. Heartbeat to a status page (opt-in)
   If the owner runs `factiii-runner status-page set` with a token for their
   own status page, the runner sends a periodic heartbeat to it: its name,
   version and time, plus this machine's Tailscale name, address and user.
   That page shows the Tailscale details only on its access-controlled admin
   view, as the owner's way back in when Factiii itself is down. This is off
   unless the owner turns it on.

8. Updating itself
   The runner checks npm for a newer @factiii/runner but installs it only
   after a person clicks "Approve update" in the Factiii app. It installs
   only a version newer than the one running. It never downloads code from
   anywhere but the npm registry.

9. Other downloads
   On request, the runner installs developer tools the owner asks for: the
   AI agent CLIs, Python packages for the local embedder (embedder/), and
   Tart (a pinned release that the runner verifies is signed by Cirrus Labs
   before installing it).


10. Connections, running services and their logs (owner only)
   Runner > Remote connections shows the runner's owner who is connected
   (each link's account, transport, start time and open terminals) and what
   the machine is serving: containers (`docker ps`), pm2 processes
   (`pm2 jlist`), the user's LaunchAgents (`launchctl list`, and each one's
   plist to see what it runs), listening ports (`lsof`) and the local
   embedder's health. On request it shows the last lines of one of those
   services' logs (`pm2 logs`, `docker logs`, or the log file a LaunchAgent
   names), at most 2,000 lines. All of it is read-only, answered only to the
   owner over their own link, and never sent to the Factiii server.

11. Who can connect, how long access lasts, and how to stop it
   Who: a link opens only for the owner and for board members the owner
   gave an access level, on a board the owner has joined. The server names
   who is connecting and their boards, and the runner checks its own grant
   for that person; if either is missing, the link is refused. Lowering a
   member's level closes their terminals and video at once. Setting them to
   None on every board closes their link. A card AI session or a job they
   already started keeps running until the owner ends it.
   How long: the runner's access token expires after 30 days and is
   renewed daily. Its refresh token changes on every use and stops working
   after 90 days unused. If an old refresh token is reused, the server
   ends the pairing, cuts live connections and alerts the owner. Terminal
   sessions have no idle timeout: they run until someone ends them from
   the app, or until the runner stops.
   How to stop it:
   - Stop the `factiii-runner start` process (Ctrl-C, or end the process).
     Nothing restarts it unless the owner installed the login service
     (item 4).
   - `factiii-runner service remove` removes the login service.
   - "Disconnect" under Settings > Runners in the Factiii app revokes the
     runner on the server, which checks this at every connection. The
     runner is told to stop its jobs and delete their containers.
   - `factiii-runner nuke` erases everything the runner holds, secrets
     included, and unpairs it.
   - `npm uninstall -g @factiii/runner` removes the package.


WHAT IT DOES NOT DO

- No code runs at install time.
- It sends no data anywhere except the Factiii servers the owner paired
  with, the owner's own opt-in status page, and the services the owner's
  agents and jobs are told to reach.
- It does not hide itself. It shows up as `factiii-runner` in process lists
  and as a named login item. `factiii-runner service remove` and
  `npm uninstall -g @factiii/runner` remove it.
- Nothing in the published code is obfuscated. dist/cli.js is an esbuild
  bundle of the TypeScript source, unminified.


